WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.642
Agent abuse occurs when an AI agent is manipulated, misused, compromised, misconfigured, or otherwise caused to behave in a way that creates security, privacy, operational, or business risk.

2
00:00:12.642 --> 00:00:23.238
Abuse can be intentional, such as a person trying to misuse an approved capability, or unintentional, such as a poorly scoped workflow acting on incorrect assumptions.

3
00:00:23.238 --> 00:00:26.142
Defenders need to account for both possibilities.

4
00:00:26.142 --> 00:00:35.291
Agents are attractive targets because they can connect model reasoning to tools, APIs, documents, memory, users, and external services.

5
00:00:35.291 --> 00:00:42.874
A normal application feature may become higher risk when an agent can perform several steps without a person reviewing each one.

6
00:00:42.874 --> 00:00:53.231
The impact is shaped by what the agent can access, what identity it uses, which actions it can perform, and how much human oversight the workflow requires.

7
00:00:53.231 --> 00:01:08.646
Organizational consequences can include unauthorized data access, incorrect system changes, inappropriate communications, financial loss, privacy exposure, service disruption, or decisions made from misleading information.

8
00:01:08.646 --> 00:01:17.347
Even a low-impact model error can become serious if the surrounding application gives it broad permissions or treats every output as trustworthy.

9
00:01:17.347 --> 00:01:22.815
Defending against abuse begins by treating the agent as a security-sensitive software system.

10
00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:33.082
Teams should define its intended purpose, allowed users, data boundaries, tools, permissions, approval requirements, logging, and safe failure behavior.

11
00:01:33.082 --> 00:01:36.793
The goal is not to assume that every user or input is hostile.

12
00:01:36.793 --> 00:01:43.987
It is to make sure the system remains controlled when people, content, models, or dependencies behave unexpectedly.
